{{also|Derrick}}
==English==
{{wp}}
[[File:StateLibQld 1 251352 Working deck of a cargo ship in port at Brisbane.jpg|thumb|Derricks on the deck of a cargo ship at Brisbane]]

===Etymology===
{{named-after|en|nat=British|occ=executioner|Thomas Derrick|wplink==}}, who invented the framework arrangement commonly known by this name to aid in the conduct of executions.  First use appears c. 1600 in the publication ''Ballad Death Earl Essex''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɛɹɪk/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Flame, not lame-derrick.wav|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛɹɪk|s=2}}
* {{hmp|en|Derek}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A device that is used for lifting and moving large objects.
#* {{quote-book
|en
|author=w:Rudyard Kipling
|title=To the City of Bombay
|year=1894
|passage=They count their ships full tale— / Their corn and oil and wine, / '''Derrick''' and loom and bale, / And rampart’s gun-flecked line; / City by City they hail: / “Hast aught to match with mine?”}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1945|month=January and February|author=T. F. Cameron|title=Dock Working|journal=Railway Magazine|pages=9, 10|text=At some places it is possible to load or discharge a vessel without any expenditure on docks or wharves, by dealing with the cargo by hand, or by the ships '''derricks''', or by means of floating discharging appliances while the vessel it moored to buoys in a tideway.  {{...}} At low tide it may be impracticable to use a ship's '''derricks''' [at a berth].}}
# A [[framework]] that is constructed over a mine or oil well for the purpose of [[bore|boring]] or lowering pipes.
#: {{synonyms|en|[[oil]] derrick}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} An [[executioner]] responsible for hanging convicts; a [[hangman]].

